# Turkey Importer — UX Simplicity Rule

Customers must not feel they are learning software architecture.

## First 10 seconds

On login, the buyer must immediately answer:

1. What are my active imports?
2. What needs my attention today?
3. What is my next single action?

## Information architecture

- Prefer few primary entries: Dashboard, My Imports, Documents, Messages.
- Freight, Customs, Delivery, Landed Cost, Exceptions open from the **import journey**, not as competing top-level products.
- Sourcing (RFQ, CommodityBid, MC, BC) must not appear in default Turkey primary nav.

## Language

Use customer words only: My Imports, Freight, Shipments, Customs, Deliveries, Documents, Landed Cost, Control Tower, Exceptions.

Do not leak: workspace, lineage, FSM, orchestration, module, entitlement (unless ops/admin).

## Density

- One next action per import card / attention item.
- No decorative CTAs. No dead ends.
- If a screen cannot answer "why does the customer need this here?", hide it, merge it, or move it into the import workspace.

## Complexity debt checklist (every UI change)

Before shipping a Turkey UI change, confirm:

- [ ] Fewer decisions than before (or equal with clearer hierarchy)
- [ ] No new jargon in customer chrome
- [ ] International Buyer chrome unchanged
- [ ] Truthful UI (no fake live tracking / fake automation)
